 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Phase Noise Handling in Amplitude-Only SLMs](15053-phase-noise-handling-in-amplitude-only-slms.md)
-A technique to model and mitigate phase noise caused by the inherent properties of liquid crystals in amplitude-only SLMs, using Jacobi-Anger expansion and Fourier series.
+This method models and mitigates phase noise inherent to liquid crystal amplitude-only Spatial Light Modulators (SLMs) by analyzing the coupling between amplitude modulation and unwanted phase modulation. It establishes a mathematical framework using Jacobi-Anger expansions to separate signal terms from noise terms in the Fourier domain, allowing for the design of grating frequencies that prevent noise overlap with the reconstructed signal. It sets it apart from sibling methods by providing a rigorous analytical derivation of noise spectra based on the specific physical properties of the liquid crystal, rather than relying solely on empirical averaging or iterative phase retrieval.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Requires calculating $N$ hologram sub-frames for each image frame, increasing computational load compared to single-frame methods [S1].
-*   **Hardware Requirements:** Requires high bandwidth and switching speed from the display (SLM) [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Avoids the high-frequency noise or speckle often included in numerically accurate, iteratively optimized holograms [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** The primary downside is that the low contrast of the noisy hologram sub-frames cannot be recovered by temporal averaging [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When high-speed SLMs are available and iterative optimization is too slow or produces excessive speckle [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When the SLM switching speed is insufficient or when high contrast is critical and cannot be tolerated in the sub-frames [S1].
